# Claims
```json
[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/config.ts",
  "line": 440,
  "title": "Keep the configured repository spelling: lowercasing it makes the queue create a duplicate repository record",
  "detail": "`loadConfig` now stores `github.queue.repos` lowercased. The queue then passes that name to `issueRunRequest` and `factory.createRun` (src/integrations/issue-queue.ts ~434 and ~359).\n\n`createRun` resolves the repository with `resolveRepo` (src/git/repos.ts), which calls `store.getRepoBySlug`, and that matches `slug = ?` case-sensitively (`repos.slug` is `TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE`, no NOCASE). With the usual configuration `repos = [\"MattFlower/limitless\"]` and the existing row `MattFlower/limitless`, the lookup for `mattflower/limitless` finds nothing (checked against a copy of a real store: 0 rows). `resolveRepo` would then run `gh repo view` and `upsertRepo` a second repository row with its own id, cache, merge policy and run history. Every queue-created run would land on it.\n\n#977 asked to canonicalize repository **keys** and to deduplicate configured names case-insensitively; it didn't ask to change the name the queue acts on. Fix:\n- Keep each configured name as written.\n- Deduplicate case-insensitively at load, keeping the first spelling.\n- Lowercase only the keys of `snapshots`, `invalidated` and similar maps.\n- Pass the configured name (or the stored repository's slug) to GraphQL, `issueRunRequest` and `createRun`.\n\nTest: with `repos = [\"Owner/Repo\", \"owner/repo\"]` and a stored repository `Owner/Repo`:\n- config yields exactly `[\"Owner/Repo\"]`;\n- a queue admission's `createRun` request uses `Owner/Repo`;\n- the store still holds one repository row.\nShow that the test fails with the current lowercasing."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/db/store.ts",
  "line": 1458,
  "title": "Don't scan every run's prompt on each poll to seed the comparison",
  "detail": "`issueRunObservations()` runs at the start of every poll. It selects every run with its `prompt` and parses `source_ref`, and the queue then calls `linkedIssues` on each row. On a copy of a real store (732 runs, 3.0 MB of prompts) that is 36\u201338 ms of synchronous work per poll: query about 25 ms, `linkedIssues` about 10 ms. #976 brought the whole poll down to 26 ms, so this more than doubles the event-loop block, and it grows with every run ever created. AGENTS.md: \"Hot paths are measured at production size.\"\n\nThe decision only needs runs that can change an issue's admission, so compare against what the poll already holds. Invalidate an issue key when a published run:\n- is the snapshot's latest run, or its owner, for that key and its `status`, `finishedAt` or linkage differs from the snapshot; or\n- is linked to that key (via `linkedIssues(msg.run)`) but isn't the snapshot's latest run for it, which covers a new or different run.\nAn accounting-only publication of a completed run (`refreshRunTotals`) then changes nothing, which is #977's second case. Before a repository's snapshot exists, keep #976's behavior for that repository.\n\nReport one before-and-after timing in the PR, using `scripts/benchmark-issue-queue.ts` or a copy of a real store at a few thousand runs. Keep #977's two regressions, and add one showing that an accounting-only publication during the poll doesn't defer admission."
 }
]
